Q: How can I prevent Windows PowerShell from breaking my bun/npm commands A: <p>I believe that the handling of spaces in the Bun shell is related to this issue:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/11328" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>oven-sh/bun</code> issue #11328 - Error calling cmd/bat scripts when both args and path contain spaces</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Until it is fixed, the issue can be worked around in various ways, but fixing the issue itself would be the best solution.</p>
<h3>Invalid test cases</h3>
<p>It appears that even when providing the full path, run into the same error.</p>
<p><strong>package.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;scripts&quot;: {
    &quot;bump&quot;: &quot;git add . &amp;&amp; 'C:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\npm.cmd' version patch -m 'Release %s' &amp;&amp; git push origin master --tags&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>PS C:\&gt; bun run bump --patch

'C:\Program' is not recognized as an internal or external command,
operable program or batch file.
error: script &quot;bump&quot; exited with code 1
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://bun.sh/docs/runtime/shell" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://bun.sh/docs/runtime/shell</a></li>
</ul>
<p>It also seems that when running via the native <code>$</code> function, we hit the same error as well.</p>
<p><strong>./scripts/bump.js</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { $ } from &quot;bun&quot;;

await $`&quot;git add .&quot;`;
await $`npm version patch -m \&quot;Release %s\&quot;&quot;`;
await $`git push origin master --tags&quot;`;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>package.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;scripts&quot;: {
    &quot;bump&quot;: &quot;bun scripts/bump.js&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>PS C:\&gt; bun run bump --patch

'C:\Program' is not recognized as an internal or external command,
operable program or batch file.
error: script &quot;bump&quot; exited with code 1
</code></pre>
<h3>Valid test cases</h3>
<p>It appears that running the three commands separately is a solution, and the issue is specifically triggered by combining <code>&amp;&amp;</code> with a path that contains spaces.</p>
<p>It appears that <code>execSync</code> in the <code>node:child_process</code> implementation is independent of <code>$</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://bun.sh/docs/runtime/nodejs-compat#nodechild_process" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://bun.sh/docs/runtime/nodejs-compat#nodechild_process</a></li>
<li><a href="https://bun.sh/reference/node/child_process/execSync" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://bun.sh/reference/node/child_process/execSync</a></li>
<li><a href="https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/blob/68f2ea4b954de73fc95677e27efb7374e894e1d7/src/js/node/child_process.ts#L647-L679" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>./src/js/nmode/child_process.ts</code> line 647 - 679 <code>execSync</code></a></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>./scripts/bump.js</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { execSync } from &quot;node:child_process&quot;;

execSync(&quot;git add .&quot;, { stdio: &quot;inherit&quot; });
execSync(&quot;npm version patch -m \&quot;Release %s\&quot;&quot;, { stdio: &quot;inherit&quot; });
execSync(&quot;git push origin master --tags&quot;, { stdio: &quot;inherit&quot; });
</code></pre>
<p><strong>package.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;scripts&quot;: {
    &quot;bump&quot;: &quot;bun scripts/bump.js&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Angular v21 SSR: validate user-agent in guard A: <p>The problem is most likely, that the <code>ServerRoutes</code> are configured with <code>RenderMode.Prerender</code> (SSG=static site generation) instead of <code>RenderMode.Server</code> (SSR).</p>
<p>This is the same, that one gets when creating an application with <code>ng new --ssr</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>// file app.routes.server.ts
export const serverRoutes: ServerRoute[] = [
  {
    path: '**',
    renderMode: RenderMode.Prerender,
  }
];
</code></pre>
<p>With SSG the rendering is static and therefor unaware of HTTP requests.</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong>: Use <code>RenderMode.Server</code> instead.</p>
<hr />
<p>Additionally, I found a problem with your approach: <code>REQUEST</code> is only available on the server side, not on the client side. The route guard will first execute on the server and then re-execute on the client, but there the condition will fail again (<code>inject(REQUEST)</code> returns null) and the wrong route chosen.</p>
<p>Let me suggest three alternative solutions while keeping your concept:</p>
<hr />
<h3>Fix 1: (simplest) Use <code>navigator.userAgent</code> as fallback</h3>
<p>I know, you didn't want it, but as additional fallback this is the easiest solution.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class DeviceDetectorService {
  private request = inject(REQUEST);
  private platformId = inject(PLATFORM_ID);

  isMobile(): boolean {
    const userAgent = this.getUserAgent();
    // your checks here
  }

  private getUserAgent(): string | undefined {
    return isPlatformServer(this.platformId)
      ? this.request?.headers.get('user-agent') || undefined
      : navigator.userAgent;
  }
</code></pre>
<h3>Fix 2: (simple) Use Separate Routes</h3>
<p>Idea: Circumvent re-execution of the route guard by redirecting to routes without guard.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>// app.routes.ts
export const routes: Routes = [{
  path: '',
  pathMatch: 'full', // &lt;-- very important!!
  loadChildren: () =&gt; desktopRoutes, // or lazy loaded, ofc.
  canMatch: [redirectToPlatform]
},{
  path: 'mobile',
  loadChildren: () =&gt; mobileRoutes, // or lazy loaded, ofc.
},{
  path: 'desktop',
  loadChildren: () =&gt; desktopRoutes, // or lazy loaded, ofc.
}];
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>// choose-platform.guard.ts
export const redirectToPlatform: CanMatchFn = (r) =&gt; {
  const router = inject(Router);
  const deviceService = inject(DeviceDetectorService);
  return deviceService.isMobile()
    ? new RedirectCommand(router.createUrlTree(['mobile']))
    : new RedirectCommand(router.createUrlTree(['desktop']))
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Fix 3 (alternative): &quot;Sync&quot; Server + Client with Cookie</h3>
<p>Idea: Let the client &quot;know&quot; about the decision on the server side through a cookie. For this use case it is probably too elaborate, but it might be a useful idea for other use-cases, therefor I share it.</p>
<p>Unfortunately this solution involves touching the non-Angular server code, however here the routes can be chosen freely, even the same route can be used.</p>
<ul>
<li>right before the <code>angularApp.handle</code> choose the platform</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>// server.ts
app.use((req, res, next) =&gt; {
  // simple approach for demo purposes, needs more fine-tuning
  const userAgent = req.get('user-agent')?.toLocaleLowerCase();
  const platform = (userAgent?.includes('mobile') || userAgent?.includes('android'))
    ? 'mobile' : 'desktop';
  res.cookie('x-platform', platform); // set the cookie for the check in the browser
  angularApp
    .handle(req, { platform: platform }) // pass request context for the check on the server
    .then((response) =&gt;
      response ? writeResponseToNodeResponse(response, res) : next(),
    )
    .catch(next);
});
</code></pre>
<p>Remarks: Here we have the opposite challenge as before: the Angular server side code must also be informed about the result. This is achieved here with the request context passed as 2nd parameter to <code>angularApp.handle</code> and later be retrieved by the injection token <code>REQUEST_CONTEXT</code>.</p>
<p>The adapted service (picks up the cookie / reads request context):</p>
<pre><code>@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root',
})
export class DeviceDetectorService {
  private platformId = inject(PLATFORM_ID);
  private requestContext = inject(REQUEST_CONTEXT) as { platform: string | undefined };

  isMobile(): boolean {
    return this.getPlatform() === 'mobile';
  }

  private getPlatform(): string | undefined {
    return isPlatformServer(this.platformId)
      ? this.requestContext?.platform  // this code will execute on server side
      : this.findCookie('x-platform'); // this code will execute on browser side
  }

  private findCookie(key: string): string | undefined {
    return document.cookie
      .split(/;\s*/)
      .find(str =&gt; str.startsWith(`${key}=`))
      ?.split('=')[1];
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Guard and routing configuration can be the same as in your code (I recommend to use <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code> for the empty routes, though).</p>
<p>I also want to add, that by injecting something from the <em>request</em> as property into a service with <code> providedIn: 'root'</code> or <code>'platform'</code>, the outcome from the first request will be &quot;sticky&quot; until the user reloads the page. Just saying it, in case you want later the user to actively switch between mobile/desktop view. Alternatively you can <em>always</em> inject directly into the route guard function.</p>

Q: Query deeply nested document with dynamic key A: <p>You <em>can</em> do this using a <code>find</code> query with a projection but it's harder to debug and a bit ugly. (I recommend using an aggregation pipeline like in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79885071/1431750">Wernfried Domscheit's answer</a>.)</p>
<ol>
<li>Use a regular <code>find</code> with the sub-objects' fields</li>
<li>Then a projection with nested <code>$map</code>'s &amp; <code>$filter</code>'s to get just the matching sub arrays for the card and user.</li>
</ol>
<p>The result is a nested list and if you expect at most one match per document, then combine those with an <code>arrayElemAt</code> with <code>0</code> to only get the first one.</p>
<pre><code>db.collection.find({
  _id: &quot;693ae6dc320b2bb92635361e&quot;,
  &quot;cards._id&quot;: &quot;600056266&quot;,
  &quot;cards.users._id&quot;: &quot;EafXiJktdKSM45OcnniU4gpcGWc2&quot;
},
{
  // all this is in the `project` part of the find query
  _id: 0,
  votes: {
    $map: {
      input: {
        $filter: {
          input: &quot;$cards&quot;,
          as: &quot;c&quot;,
          cond: { $eq: [&quot;$$c._id&quot;, &quot;600056266&quot;] }
        }
      },
      as: &quot;resultCards&quot;,
      in: {
        $map: {
          input: {
            $filter: {
              input: &quot;$$resultCards.users&quot;,
              as: &quot;u&quot;,
              cond: { $eq: [&quot;$$u._id&quot;, &quot;EafXiJktdKSM45OcnniU4gpcGWc2&quot; ] }
            }
          },
          as: &quot;user&quot;,
          in: &quot;$$user.vote&quot;
        }
      }
    }
  }
})
</code></pre>
